          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most homeowners should start with a General Tools MMD4E pin meter. It reads firewood and framing accurately, handles drywall on its building-material scale, and costs little enough to keep around for the next leak. Add a pinless Klein ET140 or General MMD7NP if you regularly check finished walls or floors, or buy the MMD950 to get both modes in one tool.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Spend more only when the number has money riding on it. A Wagner Orion 930 is worth it before a hardwood floor install or for serious woodworking, and a Protimeter or Delmhorst earns its price when you are documenting water damage. Whatever you buy, take a dry reference reading first and log your numbers over time; that habit matters more than the meter.
          </p>
